; pi4GicTimer.pi4 - ONE INTERRUPT, END TO END. THE DANGEROUS ONE.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/NotBuilding/pi4GicTimer.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -s -o _work/gictimer.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RUN pi4GicProbe.pi4 FIRST. ARM THE DEADMAN. THIS ONE CAN HANG.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; `deadman 15` before `run`, without exception. This is the first
; program in the history of this tree that clears PSTATE.I, and the
; failure modes of getting an interrupt wrong are hangs rather than
; wrong numbers:
;
;   an unquieted level interrupt   re-fires the instant the handler
;                                  returns, forever, with no output
;   a vector table that is wrong   branches somewhere and stops
;   an eret that does not restore  never comes back
;   a handler that faults          fault.pi4 reports it and parks,
;                                  which is the good outcome
;
; Every one of those is a silent board. The deadman is the only way
; back and there is no U-Boot any more.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS ANSWERS THAT NO MODEL CAN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   1. Does an IRQ actually arrive at VBAR_EL2 + $280, and does the
;      vector entry there record slot 5?
;   2. Does the CNTHP comparator's output actually reach the GIC as
;      PPI 10 / INTID 26 on this silicon? The device tree says it does
;      (bcm2711.dtsi:444-453). The device tree describes the
;      configuration LINUX sets up, and armstub8.S:92-102 programs the
;      BCM2711's OTHER local-interrupt block on the way past. Only the
;      board can settle it.
;   3. Does GICC_IAR return 26, and does GICC_EOIR end it, so that a
;      SECOND interrupt can be taken?
;   4. Does `eret` out of an IRQ restore DAIF such that interrupts are
;      still enabled afterwards?
;   5. Does the tick keep time - is the measured interval anywhere
;      near the requested one?
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS, AND WHY THIS FILE IS MOSTLY THEM
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; A probe made only of positive cases cannot tell "the interrupt
; works" from "something calls my callback". Both controls are timed
; waits over the SAME number of periods as the positive case, so
; "nothing happened" means "nothing happened in a window where
; something demonstrably does happen", not "nothing happened yet".
;
;   CONTROL A - ARMED BUT MASKED.
;       The timer is started and the GIC line is claimed and enabled,
;       and PSTATE.I is STILL SET. Wait ten periods. THE TICK COUNT
;       MUST BE ZERO.
;
;       This is the control that separates a real interrupt from a
;       fixture. In a model that "delivers an interrupt because you
;       told it to", the callback runs here. On hardware, and in a
;       model that implements PSTATE.I, it does not - the interrupt is
;       pending in the distributor the whole time and the core is
;       simply not taking it. Step 6 then proves it was pending, by
;       reading it back out of GICD_ISPENDR before the unmask.
;
;   CONTROL B - STOPPED.
;       After the positive case, GicTickStop() disables the timer and
;       releases the line, WITHOUT re-masking the core. Wait ten more
;       periods. THE TICK COUNT MUST NOT MOVE.
;
;       Interrupts are still enabled here on purpose. If the count
;       moves, either the source was never really the timer or the
;       line was never really released, and either way the positive
;       result above means less than it looks.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE THREE COUNTERS THAT MUST AGREE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; They are kept by three different pieces of code that do not read
; each other, so agreement is evidence and disagreement names the
; layer that is wrong:
;
;   FaultIrqCount()   fault.pi4    exceptions taken in an IRQ slot
;   GicIrqCount()     gic.pi4      IARs dispatched to a handler
;   ticks             this file    times the callback ran
;
; FaultIrqCount() larger than GicIrqCount() means interrupts are
; arriving that the GIC has no handler for - check
; GicUnclaimedCount() and GicSpuriousCount(). GicIrqCount() larger
; than ticks means the tick ISR saw ISTATUS clear and declined, which
; means something other than the comparator is asserting INTID 26.
;
; AND FaultCount() MUST BE ZERO. That is fault.pi4's count of real
; exceptions - aborts, undefined instructions, traps. A non-zero value
; means something faulted inside the handler and the run is void
; whatever the tick count says.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IF IT HANGS, WHERE IT STOPPED IS THE ANSWER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every step announces itself and drains the UART before attempting
; anything, the pattern pi4FaultProbe.pi4 settled on.
;
;   stops after "unmask"        The first interrupt arrived and did
;                               not come back. Either the vector slot
;                               is wrong, or the eret is, or the tick
;                               ISR is not quieting the comparator and
;                               the board is in the storm.
;   stops after "armed, masked" GicTickStart or GicClaim touched
;                               something that faulted, with no vector
;                               installed to say what. Unlikely - the
;                               vectors go in before this.
;   prints a FAULT line         fault.pi4 caught it. Read the ESR
;                               against fault.pi4's header. This is
;                               the design working.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/fault.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gic.pi4"

#PR_MAX = 80
Global Dim pr_val.i[#PR_MAX]
Global pr_n.i

; The period. 1000 microseconds is a 1 kHz tick - fast enough that ten
; periods is ten milliseconds and the whole probe is imperceptible,
; slow enough that fifty-four thousand counter ticks separate them and
; a handler taking a few hundred cycles cannot overrun.
#TICK_US    = 1000
#WAIT_TICKS = 10

; Set by the interrupt. A Global because that is the only thing a
; handler and a main program can share, and because a local would let
; the compiler decide the loop below has no side effects.
Global pr_ticks.i
Global pr_firstAt.i
Global pr_lastAt.i

; The tick number pr_lastAt belongs to, written by the SAME interrupt
; that wrote pr_lastAt.
;
; IT IS NOT pr_ticks READ AFTERWARDS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A REAL BUG
; THIS PROBE HAD. The first version divided the deadline span by
; (pr_ticks - 1) where pr_ticks had been sampled a dozen UART rows
; earlier - and interrupts keep arriving while a row is being printed,
; so the numerator and the denominator came from different moments and
; the answer was 48413 counter ticks per period instead of 54000. It
; looked exactly like a drifting comparator. Two values that are
; divided by each other have to be sampled together, and the only
; place they can be is inside the handler.
Global pr_lastN.i

; THE CALLBACK. Deliberately tiny. It runs on fault.pi4's 1 KiB
; private handler stack with the machine mid-interrupt, so it does the
; least that is still useful and prints NOTHING - a UART write from an
; ISR at 1 kHz would be its own experiment.
;
; It timestamps the first and last tick so that the interval can be
; checked afterwards, on the ordinary stack, at leisure.
Procedure Tick()
  pr_ticks = pr_ticks + 1
  If pr_ticks = 1
    pr_firstAt = GicTickNext()
  EndIf
  pr_lastAt = GicTickNext()
  pr_lastN  = pr_ticks
EndProcedure

; The reporter fault.pi4 calls if anything goes wrong. It should never
; run. If it does, its output is the finding and everything below it
; is void.
Procedure FaultReport()
  UartWriteStr("   [FAULT slot ")
  UartWrite(48 + (FaultSlot() / 10))
  UartWrite(48 + (FaultSlot() - (FaultSlot() / 10) * 10))
  UartWriteStr(" esr ")
  UartWriteStr(FaultClassText(FaultEc()))
  UartWriteStr("]")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Rec(*label, v.i)
  If pr_n < #PR_MAX
    pr_val[pr_n] = v
    pr_n = pr_n + 1
  EndIf
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Say(*label)
  UartWriteStr("-- ")
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWriteNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

; Spin for n tick periods, measured on the architectural counter.
;
; IT DOES NOT USE delay(). timer.pi4 would do, and this file does not
; include it: a library never includes a library and this is a
; program, so it could - but the wait has to be readable next to the
; thing it is waiting for, and GicTickDelta() is already the exact
; number of counter ticks in one period. Using the same number for the
; wait and for the timer means "ten periods" cannot drift away from
; what the timer thinks a period is.
;
; SUBTRACT, NEVER COMPARE AGAINST A DEADLINE. Style on this target
; rather than a hazard - .i is 64 bits and the counter wraps in 292000
; years - but the habit is the fleet's and it costs nothing.
Procedure SpinPeriods(n.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define want.i
  t0 = GicReadCntpct()
  want = GicTickDelta() * n
  While (GicReadCntpct() - t0) < want
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define rc.i
  Define before.i
  Define pendingWhileMasked.i
  Define span.i
  Define bIMasked.i
  Define bTicks.i
  Define bFaultIrq.i
  Define bGicIrq.i
  Define bTickCount.i
  Define bLastId.i
  Define bLastIar.i
  Define bOverruns.i
  Define bSpurious.i
  Define bUnclaimed.i
  Define bFaults.i
  Define bNested.i
  Define bFirst.i
  Define bLast.i
  Define bLastN.i

  UartInit(115200)
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("pi4GicTimer - one interrupt, end to end")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  1. VECTORS FIRST. ALWAYS.
  ;
  ;  Nothing below may run without a vector table, and the ORDER is
  ;  the contract: vectors, then the seam, then the controller, then
  ;  the unmask. gic.pi4's GicEnableInterrupts() refuses if VBAR_EL2
  ;  does not hold one, so getting this wrong is a refusal rather than
  ;  a hang - but the refusal is a backstop, not the plan.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("install vectors")
  FaultSetReporter(@FaultReport)
  Rec("fault install   ", FaultInstall())
  Rec("fault err       ", FaultError())
  Rec("VBAR            ", GicVbar())
  Rec("VBAR and 2047   ", GicVbar() & 2047)

  If FaultInstalled() = 0
    UartWriteStr("REFUSED: ")
    UartWriteStr(FaultErrorText())
    UartWriteNl()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  2. THE SEAM. One line, and it is the whole of the wiring between
  ;     the two libraries - neither includes the other.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("seam")
  FaultSetIrqHandler(@GicIrqEntry)
  Rec("irq handler set ", FaultIrqHandler())

  ; Mask, whatever the loader left. Everything from here to the unmask
  ; depends on it and the compiler's --entry-returns startup does not
  ; do it for us (EmitCore_A64.pbi:230-234).
  GicDisableInterrupts()
  Rec("I masked        ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  3. THE CONTROLLER
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("GicInit")
  rc = GicInit()
  Rec("init rc         ", rc)
  Rec("init err        ", GicError())
  Rec("dist IIDR       ", GicDistIidr())
  Rec("cpu IIDR        ", GicCpuIidr())
  If rc = 0
    UartWriteStr("REFUSED: ")
    UartWriteStr(GicErrorText())
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("Run pi4GicProbe.pi4 - it explains every refusal row.")
    UartWriteNl()
    FaultUninstall()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The group witness, before anything is armed. If this fails there
  ; is no point going on: the line is invisible to us and the timer
  ; would tick into a controller that cannot forward it.
  Say("group witness")
  Rec("witness rc      ", GicGroupWitness(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("HPPIR saw       ", GicGroupWitnessSaw())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4. ARM THE TIMER - STILL MASKED
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("armed, masked")
  Rec("tick start      ", GicTickStart(#TICK_US, @Tick))
  Rec("tick err        ", GicError())
  Rec("CNTFRQ          ", GicTickHz())
  Rec("ticks per period", GicTickDelta())
  Rec("CNTHP_CTL       ", GicTickCtl())
  Rec("line enabled    ", GicLineEnabled(GicTickIntId()))

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  5. CONTROL A - the count MUST stay zero while I is set
  ;
  ;  Read THE TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS in the header. This is the row
  ;  that separates a real interrupt from a fixture calling a
  ;  callback, and it is the reason this file is worth running rather
  ;  than reasoning about.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("control A - masked wait")
  SpinPeriods(#WAIT_TICKS)
  Rec("A ticks         ", pr_ticks)
  Rec("A fault irqs    ", FaultIrqCount())
  Rec("A gic irqs      ", GicIrqCount())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  6. AND IT WAS PENDING THE WHOLE TIME
  ;
  ;  Without this row, control A is ambiguous: "no interrupt arrived"
  ;  would be equally consistent with "the timer never fired at all",
  ;  which would make the positive case below prove nothing about
  ;  masking. GICD_ISPENDR says the distributor is holding it.
  ;
  ;  ISTATUS in CNTHP_CTL is the same fact read from the other end -
  ;  the comparator saying its condition is met - so the two together
  ;  span the whole path from the timer to the distributor with the
  ;  core still deaf.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  pendingWhileMasked = GicLinePending(GicTickIntId())
  Rec("A line pending  ", pendingWhileMasked)
  Rec("A CNTHP ISTATUS ", (GicTickCtl() >> 2) & 1)
  Rec("A HPPIR         ", GicHighestPending())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  7. THE POSITIVE CASE
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("unmask")
  Rec("enable rc       ", GicEnableInterrupts())
  Rec("enable err      ", GicError())
  Rec("I masked after  ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)

  SpinPeriods(#WAIT_TICKS)

  ; STILL ENABLED? Question 4 in the header, and it is read FIRST,
  ; before the snapshot below masks anything. If the eret did not
  ; restore PSTATE.I from SPSR we would have taken exactly one
  ; interrupt and then gone deaf, so this row and "B ticks" together
  ; answer it. Reading it after a deliberate mask/unmask would only
  ; prove that GicEnableInterrupts() works, which is a different
  ; question and one that is already answered by "enable rc".
  bIMasked = (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE SNAPSHOT, AND WHY IT IS TAKEN WITH INTERRUPTS OFF
  ;
  ;  ***THIS WAS A REAL BUG IN THIS PROBE AND IT IS WORTH THE
  ;  PARAGRAPH.*** The first version simply printed each counter as it
  ;  went. Printing a row over the UART takes long enough for another
  ;  interrupt to arrive, so by the time "gic irqs" was read it was
  ;  larger than the "ticks" printed two rows above - and the gate
  ;  reported that three counters which MUST agree did not. They did
  ;  agree; they were just never looked at together.
  ;
  ;  The same defect made the tick interval come out at 43714 counter
  ;  ticks instead of 54000, because the span and the tick number it
  ;  was divided by were sampled six rows apart. That number looked
  ;  exactly like a drifting comparator.
  ;
  ;  Any set of counters that will be COMPARED WITH EACH OTHER has to
  ;  be read in one go, with the thing that changes them held off.
  ;  That is what GicDisableInterrupts() is for, and this is the
  ;  smallest honest critical section in the tree.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  GicDisableInterrupts()
  bTicks     = pr_ticks
  bFaultIrq  = FaultIrqCount()
  bGicIrq    = GicIrqCount()
  bTickCount = GicTickCount()
  bLastId    = GicLastIntId()
  bLastIar   = GicLastIar()
  bOverruns  = GicTickOverruns()
  bSpurious  = GicSpuriousCount()
  bUnclaimed = GicUnclaimedCount()
  bFaults    = FaultCount()
  bNested    = FaultIrqNested()
  bFirst     = pr_firstAt
  bLast      = pr_lastAt
  bLastN     = pr_lastN
  GicEnableInterrupts()

  Say("after the wait")
  Rec("B ticks         ", bTicks)
  Rec("B fault irqs    ", bFaultIrq)
  Rec("B gic irqs      ", bGicIrq)
  Rec("B gic tickcount ", bTickCount)
  Rec("B last INTID    ", bLastId)
  Rec("B last IAR      ", bLastIar)
  Rec("B overruns      ", bOverruns)
  Rec("B spurious      ", bSpurious)
  Rec("B unclaimed     ", bUnclaimed)
  Rec("B faults        ", bFaults)
  Rec("B irq nested    ", bNested)
  Rec("B I masked      ", bIMasked)

  ; The measured interval, in counter ticks, between the first tick's
  ; deadline and the last one's. It is (lastN-1) * delta EXACTLY,
  ; because the ISR advances the deadline arithmetically rather than
  ; from the counter - which makes this a check on the ARITHMETIC, not
  ; on the part's timekeeping. Real drift would show up as overruns.
  span = bLast - bFirst
  Rec("B first deadline", bFirst)
  Rec("B last deadline ", bLast)
  Rec("B last tick no  ", bLastN)
  Rec("B span          ", span)
  If bLastN > 1
    Rec("B span per tick ", span / (bLastN - 1))
  Else
    Rec("B span per tick ", 0)
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  8. THE STORM GUARD - fire a line that has no handler
  ;
  ;  Read gic.pi4's section WHY AN UNCLAIMED LINE IS DISABLED. An
  ;  interrupt with nobody to service it is the classic way to hang a
  ;  board, and the guard against it is one write that nothing else in
  ;  this probe exercises. A guard that is never tested is a guard
  ;  nobody knows is broken.
  ;
  ;  INTID 27 is the virtual timer's PPI (bcm2711.dtsi:450-451). It is
  ;  not claimed, nothing arms the virtual timer, and this is the only
  ;  place in the tree that touches it.
  ;
  ;  THE TICK IS STILL RUNNING FOR THIS STEP, ON PURPOSE. If the
  ;  unclaimed path failed to write GICC_EOIR, the CPU interface would
  ;  keep INTID 27's priority as the running priority and NO FURTHER
  ;  INTERRUPT WOULD EVER BE SIGNALLED - the tick would simply stop.
  ;  So "S ticks moved" is how this step proves the EOI happened,
  ;  without needing a second unclaimed interrupt to prove it with.
  ;
  ;  A software-pended interrupt is edge-like - IAR clears the pending
  ;  bit and nothing re-asserts it - so this cannot produce an actual
  ;  storm. What it proves is that the line is counted, named and
  ;  DISABLED, which is the part of the guard that stops a real one.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("storm guard")
  before = pr_ticks
  Rec("S 27 enabled    ", GicEnableLine(27))
  Rec("S pend rc       ", GicSetPending(27))
  SpinPeriods(2)
  Rec("S unclaimed     ", GicUnclaimedCount())
  Rec("S last unclaimed", GicLastUnclaimed())
  Rec("S 27 still en   ", GicLineEnabled(27))
  Rec("S 27 pending    ", GicLinePending(27))
  Rec("S 27 active     ", GicLineActive(27))
  If pr_ticks > before
    Rec("S ticks moved   ", 1)
  Else
    Rec("S ticks moved   ", 0)
  EndIf
  Rec("S faults        ", FaultCount())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  9. CONTROL B - stopped, but STILL UNMASKED
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("control B - stopped wait")
  GicTickStop()
  before = pr_ticks
  Rec("C ticks at stop ", before)
  Rec("C line enabled  ", GicLineEnabled(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("C CNTHP_CTL     ", GicTickCtl())
  Rec("C I masked      ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)

  SpinPeriods(#WAIT_TICKS)

  Rec("C ticks after   ", pr_ticks)
  Rec("C delta         ", pr_ticks - before)
  Rec("C unclaimed     ", GicUnclaimedCount())
  Rec("C spurious      ", GicSpuriousCount())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; 10. HAND THE BOARD BACK, IN THE REVERSE ORDER
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("shutdown")
  GicDisableInterrupts()
  GicShutdown()
  FaultSetIrqHandler(0)
  FaultUninstall()

  Rec("gic ready       ", GicReady())
  Rec("fault installed ", FaultInstalled())
  Rec("irq handler     ", FaultIrqHandler())
  Rec("exit I masked   ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)
  Rec("total faults    ", FaultCount())
  Rec("results         ", pr_n)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("PASS looks like: A ticks $0, A line pending $1,")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("B ticks $A or more, B last INTID $1A, B I masked $0,")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("B span per tick $D2F0, S unclaimed $1, S 27 still en $0,")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("S ticks moved $1, C delta $0, total faults $0.")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("B overruns $1 IS EXPECTED and is not a defect: control A")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("deliberately held the tick masked for ten periods, so the")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("first one taken after the unmask is ten periods late and")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("the ISR resynchronises rather than trying to catch up.")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("Anything else, read the header - every row has a paragraph.")
  UartWriteNl()

  UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
